```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: red
REAL_USE_CASE: Agent serving systems that issue multiple tool calls per step (e.g., ReAct-style loops, function-calling agents) could benefit from parallel dispatch, but ONLY when dependencies are known at plan time. The naive post-hoc detect-then-parallelize approach has no safe adopter: measured FNR e=0.164 (state-aware heuristic) to 0.615 (Qwen2.5-1.5B LLM) yields empirical f_wrong=3.1-16.7% of sequences (2.4-8.5% of calls), with missed stateful edges causing wrong CWD/missing file -> hard failure. LLMCompiler (arXiv:2312.04511) demonstrates the viable path: its planner emits an explicit DAG with variable refs ($1,$2...), never performing post-hoc independence detection, achieving reported 3.7x latency speedup.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only. BFCL v4 parallel categories show f_indep=1.000 because they are definitionally curated to be parallelizable (RESULTS.md §2, honest caveat: "curated ceiling, NOT a population mix"); L0's 0.44-0.71 mixed-corpus fraction is more representative of heterogeneous workloads. The safety wall dominates: even the best detector (heuristic data-flow + CWD-state-aware) has e=0.164, >6x the 0.025 safety threshold and ≥ L0's assumed 0.05. Empirical f_wrong=0.024 per-call (0.031 per-sequence) for that detector, 0.085/0.167 for value-only heuristic, 0.076/0.148 for LLM (RESULTS.md §4). The L0 binomial 1-(1-e)^k is validated (modeled 0.074 vs empirical 0.024-0.085). The "free latency win" does NOT survive production stateful traces.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [1] Safety threshold miss: best e=0.164 (6.5x over 0.025), LLM e=0.615 (24x over); no tested detector approaches safety. [2] State invisibility: FNR 0.273-0.970 on stateful/order deps (no visible argument link) vs 0.00-0.30 on data-flow (RESULTS.md §3); the danger is precisely dependencies with NO arg link. [3] Architecture mismatch: requires plan-time DAG construction (LLMCompiler style); cannot retrofit onto existing serial ReAct loops that emit flat call sets. [4] Benchmark bias: BFCL parallel split is a curated ceiling, not a production mix of stateful and parallel ops. [5] Correctness cost: 3-17% sequence corruption is a hard fail (wrong file/state), not a soft latency/quality trade-off.
RATIONALE: L1 measured real e=0.582 (heuristic value-dataflow), 0.164 (state-aware heuristic), 0.615 (LLM Qwen2.5-1.5B) on 257 real BFCL multi-turn DAGs (110 true edges) — all >>0.025 and ≥0.05 (RESULTS.md §3). State-class FNR dominates: 0.970 (heuristic), 0.273 (state-aware), 0.818 (LLM) vs data-class FNR 0.00-0.30 (RESULTS.md §3). Empirical f_wrong computed from real detector errors: 0.024-0.085 per-call, 0.031-0.167 per-sequence (RESULTS.md §4). f_indep=1.0 on BFCL parallel categories is a curated ceiling, not population (RESULTS.md §2, honest caveat). LLMCompiler (arXiv:2312.04511) uses plan-time DAG with variable refs, not post-hoc detection, reporting up to 3.7x latency speedup (RESULTS.md §5); the measured-e wall explains that design choice.
```
